    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d138">

                <pb facs="0391" n="247" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg247"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">861.77 Chinese Eastern/140: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in China</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>)<anchor n="23" xml:id="d138-f1"
                    /></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-07-26T14:00:00-04:00">July 26, 1929—2
                    p.m.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p>248. On July 25 I called in the Japanese, French, British, and Italian
                    Ambassadors and the German Chargé d’Affaires and handed them the following <hi
                        rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi>, with accompanying suggestions
                    concerning a commission of conciliation, which have been communicated by them to
                    their Governments. Since premature publicity would imperil the proposal’s being
                    carried out successfully, it was agreed to keep the matter strictly secret for
                    the present.</p>
                <p>[Here follows in full the <hi rendition="#italic">aide-mémoire</hi> and
                    enclosure, printed on <ref target="#pg242">page 242</ref>.]</p>
                <p>Repeat as No. 71 to the Embassy in Japan.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Stimson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d138-f1" n="23" type="source" xml:id="d138-f1-content">
                <p>See last paragraph for instructions to repeat to the Embassy in Japan. The same
                    telegram, except for the last sentence, sent to the Ambassador in France (No.
                    249), with instructions to repeat to the Ambassadors in Germany (No. 53), Great
                    Britain (No. 191), and Italy (No. 54).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
